From 218eeacecbac4e23b3b2e0c2a59e6e54c94db58c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: airebuild <k@local>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 13:33:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(rules): stop persistent conditions from alerting every
 morning

The pest rule fired 7 days out of 7 on ordinary Hazaribagh monsoon
weather, at SEVERE, every day, for the whole season.

Two separate causes.

1. Severity. severity_for() divides a value by a threshold, which assumes
   the value can keep climbing. The pest rule fed it a count of days
   inside a 7-day forecast, which saturates at 7. Against a threshold of
   3, any run of 6+ days was automatically SEVERE. Pest severity is now
   capped at MODERATE: "go and scout your field" is useful advice, but it
   does not belong at the same level as "your crop is about to be under
   water".

2. Cadence. event_id hashes (farm_id, rule_id, window_start) and
   save_advisory upserts on advisory_id, so a window that starts at
   `today` mints a fresh advisory every morning. Pest and dry spell both
   describe conditions that persist for weeks, so both now anchor their
   window to the Monday of the current ISO week. The advisory refreshes
   in place and re-issues next week. Rules that fire on genuinely
   discrete days (heat spell, harvest rain, frost) already anchor to
   those days and are unchanged.

Verified end to end against 14 consecutive monsoon mornings on one farm:
14 SEVERE advisories before, 2 MODERATE after.

What this does NOT fix: the thresholds themselves. PEST_HUMIDITY_PCT at
85% with a paddy t_min band spanning a full 10 degrees describes the
monsoon, not a pest signal. A correct rule would measure departure from
normal for the week, and baselines.json has no humidity baseline to
compare against. Inventing 35-year percentiles to close that gap would be
precisely the fabricated provenance this project exists to avoid, so the
gap is documented in the rule and left for an agronomist.

10 new tests (133 total, was 123) pinning the week anchor, one-event-id-
per-week for both rules, re-issue on the following week, and the monsoon
trigger measurement itself so a future retune is noticed.
